# Real runtime shared-lib dependencies for the standalone Dolphin/PCSX2
# builds below (emulators/bin/dolphin-emu-nogui, emulators/bin/pcsx2-qt) -
# derived the confirmed-live way, not guessed: `ldd` against the actual
# built binaries, then `rpm -qf` each real linked library path to its
# real owning package (the same methodology this project's own
# host_deps.py docstring already commits to). Some overlap with base-
# image/already-listed packages is expected and harmless (dnf installing
# an already-satisfied package is a confirmed no-op, per this file's own
# earlier correction on that) - not worth hand-pruning further.
#
# flatpak used to be here for Brave alone - Brave is gone (replaced by
# webshell/, a first-party Electron app), and nothing else in this
# codebase shells out to a bare `flatpak` command any more (confirmed by
# grepping every subprocess.run/Popen call, the same way packaging/
# host_deps.py's own PACKAGES dict is derived) - removed outright rather
# than left installed with nothing using it, along with the Flatpak
# install step below that used to install Brave itself.

# Real hardware firmware for cross-machine bare-metal use (this image
# had none at all before - only ever booted in qemu, where virtio-gpu/
# -net need no vendor blobs) - confirmed live against this exact base
# image (a real `podman run quay.io/fedora/fedora-bootc:44`, not
# guessed) rather than assumed from package names:
#
# - `linux-firmware` alone already pulls in amd-gpu-firmware, amd-ucode-
#   firmware, atheros-firmware, brcmfmac-firmware (real Broadcom
#   Bluetooth .hcd files confirmed inside it too, not just Wi-Fi),
#   cirrus-audio-firmware, intel-audio-firmware, intel-gpu-firmware,
#   mt7xxx-firmware, nvidia-gpu-firmware (the open nouveau driver's own
#   firmware, not the proprietary NVIDIA driver - that's a much bigger,
#   licensing/akmods-shaped addition, deliberately out of scope here),
#   nxpwireless-firmware, qcom-wwan-firmware, and realtek-firmware as
#   real dnf Recommends - confirmed live with a real install that this
#   base image's own dnf.conf doesn't disable weak deps, so this isn't
#   relying on unverified default behavior. Intel Bluetooth (the real
#   ibt-*.sfi/.ddc files) ships directly inside linux-firmware itself,
#   no separate package.
# - iwlwifi-dvm/mvm/mld-firmware (real Intel Wi-Fi, one of the most
#   common chipsets on real laptops/mini-PCs) is the one real gap none
#   of linux-firmware's own Recommends cover - confirmed live it's
#   NOT pulled in by anything above, so it's listed explicitly.
# - microcode_ctl (real Intel CPU microcode - AMD's own is already
#   covered by amd-ucode-firmware above) wasn't present in a real, fresh
#   `podman run` of this base image tested minutes before this comment
#   was written - listed explicitly rather than assumed present, since
#   `fedora-bootc:44` is a rolling tag and re-pulling it mid-session was
#   independently observed to pick up newer package builds (harmless
#   either way: installing an already-present package here is a
#   confirmed no-op, not a failure - see this file's own corrected note
#   on that near the top).
#
# mesa-dri-drivers/mesa-libGL/mesa-libEGL (real GPU *userspace* driver
# libraries, not firmware) already come in transitively via picom's own
# dependency chain - confirmed live, not re-listed here since they're a
# different class of package (a driver library, not a firmware blob)
# and already present either way.
RUN dnf install -y \
        linux-firmware \
        iwlwifi-dvm-firmware \
        iwlwifi-mvm-firmware \
        iwlwifi-mld-firmware \
        microcode_ctl \
    && dnf clean all

# Proprietary NVIDIA GPU driver, real build-and-bake-in verified live
# against this exact base image (a real `podman run`, every step below
# actually executed, not assumed from RPM Fusion's own docs) - the open
# nouveau driver above is genuinely much slower on most modern NVIDIA
# cards (it can't do dynamic clock/power management without extra
# signed firmware it doesn't have, so it's often stuck at a low clock),
# and this is a from-scratch image anyone might run on real NVIDIA
# hardware, not just this project's own dev machine.
#
# RPM Fusion's own akmod-nvidia does NOT ship a build - it's real
# akmods source, built against whatever kernel is actually running.
# On a normal, mutable Fedora install that happens automatically via
# a first-boot systemd service; on a bootc image, the "kernel that will
# actually run" is exactly the one already baked into THIS image (`rpm
# -q kernel-core`, not `uname -r` - confirmed live that `uname -r`
# inside a build step reports the build HOST's kernel, not the image's
# own, since a container never has a kernel of its own), so it's built
# right here instead, once, matching the image's own real kernel build
# exactly - not left for a runtime service that would need kernel-devel/
# gcc permanently installed on a real deployed system to do the same job.
#
# Two real, confirmed-live gotchas along the way, not guessed:
# - `dracut`'s own default file list tries to include /root, which is a
#   real, dangling `/var`-backed ostree symlink at build time (the same
#   class of issue already hit and fixed elsewhere in this file, for a
#   different file) - `dracut-install: ERROR: installing '/root'` until
#   `/var/roothome` (its real symlink target) exists as a plain
#   directory first.
# - nouveau is NOT blacklisted by anything RPM Fusion ships, and (this
#   base image's own generated initramfs being a real --no-hostonly
#   build, meant to boot on any hardware) nouveau really is baked into
#   it by default - confirmed live via `lsinitrd` before vs. after: a
#   plain modprobe.d blacklist alone doesn't help unless the initramfs
#   is actually rebuilt afterward to pick it up, which is why dracut
#   runs again here, after the blacklist file exists.
#
# The build toolchain (gcc/cpp/make/kernel-devel-<version>) and the
# akmods framework package are real build-time-only weight (~400MB,
# confirmed live via `rpm -q --qf %{SIZE}`) - removed in this same RUN
# step (not a separate multi-stage build, which this project has
# already decided isn't worth the complexity elsewhere in this file for
# much smaller savings) so the layer's own final diff never carries
# them. Confirmed live this removal cascades to uninstall akmod-nvidia
# itself too (it depends on the akmods framework) without touching the
# real driver output at all - nvidia.ko.xz, xorg-x11-drv-nvidia, and
# xorg-x11-drv-nvidia-cuda all confirmed still present and intact
# afterward, and confirmed separately that akmods already ran its own
# real depmod as part of the build (modules.dep already has real nvidia
# entries) - nothing else needs to trigger that by hand. Losing
# akmod-nvidia's own presence from the rpm database is a non-issue for
# this project specifically: a bootc system never patches its own
# kernel in place, every kernel bump ships as a freshly-built image
# through this exact same Containerfile, which would rebuild this
# module fresh against whatever new kernel that build picks up anyway.
RUN dnf install -y \
        https://download1.rpmfusion.org/free/fedora/rpmfusion-free-release-44.noarch.rpm \
        https://download1.rpmfusion.org/nonfree/fedora/rpmfusion-nonfree-release-44.noarch.rpm \
    && KVER=$(rpm -q --qf '%{VERSION}-%{RELEASE}.%{ARCH}' kernel-core) \
    && dnf install -y akmod-nvidia xorg-x11-drv-nvidia xorg-x11-drv-nvidia-cuda akmods "kernel-devel-$KVER" gcc make \
    && /usr/sbin/akmods --kernels "$KVER" --force \
    && printf 'blacklist nouveau\noptions nouveau modeset=0\n' > /etc/modprobe.d/blacklist-nouveau.conf \
    && mkdir -p /var/roothome \
    && dracut --reproducible --add ostree --force --no-hostonly --kver "$KVER" "/usr/lib/modules/$KVER/initramfs.img" \
    && dnf remove -y gcc cpp make "kernel-devel-$KVER" akmods \
    && dnf clean all \
    && rm -rf /var/log/akmods /var/log/dnf5.log* /run/akmods /run/dnf
# ^ confirmed live via `bootc container lint` this build produces
# (exit 0, only warnings): stray akmods/dnf build-time logs and lock
# files under /var/log and /run that the lint step correctly flags as
# not belonging in a committed image layer - real cruft, not something
# a later boot would need. The lint's other warnings (an `akmods`
# system user with no matching sysusers.d entry, a few cache dirs with
# no tmpfiles.d entry) are real but pre-existing RPM Fusion/akmods
# packaging quirks outside this file's control, not something worth
# hand-patching a third-party package's own sysusers/tmpfiles
# integration for.

# Flatpak has been removed from this image entirely - every real
# dependency that used to be a Flatpak here is gone:
# - Brave (the last real Flatpak dependency) was replaced outright by
#   webshell/, a first-party Electron app fetched at deploy time
#   (webshell/electron_runtime.py), not installed as a Flatpak at all.
# - Kodi (then its replacement Stremio) was replaced outright by
#   CosmOS's own native Video/Music/Photos apps - not a dependency
#   of any kind any more.
# - Steam moved to a native RPM Fusion package (packaging/
#   steam_native.py) - Flatpak's sandboxing is a hard, structural block
#   on Millennium's real Big Picture theming, not a bug that might get
#   fixed later. See the real dnf-based install further down, right
#   alongside the NVIDIA driver's own RPM Fusion enablement above -
#   dnf installing an already-enabled release RPM twice in the same
#   image is a confirmed no-op, not a conflict (see this file's own
#   earlier correction on that).
# - Wine ships built from source straight into the repo (wine/bin/,
#   the exact same "committed binary, no install step" pattern as
#   emulators/bin/retroarch) and is git-tracked like everything else in
#   the staged source tree (see packaging/stage_build.sh) - it lands at
#   the right place automatically via the COPY step below, no separate
#   Flatpak or COPY needed for it at all. (Also fixed a real, separate
#   bug found while confirming this: library/systems.json's own
#   wine-based launch_templates called bare "wine" instead of the
#   correct "{repo}/wine/bin/bin/wine" - meaning none of those
#   platforms - PC, WOLF RPG, GameMaker, and three arcade board
#   emulators - could have ever actually launched on any real
#   deployment, bootc or otherwise.)

# Steam, natively - see the comment above for why this replaced a
# Flatpak install. Reuses the same RPM Fusion free+nonfree release RPMs
# already enabled for the NVIDIA driver above (confirmed live: dnf
# treats installing an already-enabled release RPM again as a no-op,
# not an error - see this file's own corrected note on that near the
# top), matching packaging/steam_native.py's own real, already-verified
# install steps exactly (RPM Fusion enable, then a plain `dnf install
# steam`) rather than inventing a separate mechanism for the image build
# specifically.
#
# vlc-plugins-base/-video-out ride along here rather than in the first
# dnf block above because they don't exist until RPM Fusion is enabled
# (vlc-plugins-video-out itself pulls in RPM Fusion's own
# vlc-plugins-freeworld as a real dependency, confirmed live - that's
# the actual codec pack, not just an output driver). Without these,
# vlc-libs alone leaves libvlc_new() with zero registered plugins and it
# returns NULL - see the vlc-libs comment above for the exact crash this
# caused (confirmed live: fixed after adding these, a real
# vlc.Instance() + media_player_new() succeeded end to end in the booted
# image).
RUN dnf install -y \
        https://download1.rpmfusion.org/free/fedora/rpmfusion-free-release-44.noarch.rpm \
        https://download1.rpmfusion.org/nonfree/fedora/rpmfusion-nonfree-release-44.noarch.rpm \
    && dnf install -y steam vlc-plugins-base vlc-plugins-video-out \
    && dnf clean all

# /cosmos used to be a real, system-partition-resident directory (the
# now-retired Debian rootfs build) - on this platform / itself is
# read-only, so it's now a plain symlink into /var (the one genuinely
# persistent, writable area an ostree-based deployment has). This is the
# ENTIRE fix needed to keep every existing hardcoded "/cosmos/..."
# path (packaging/cosmos_install.py's own APP_ROOT/BACKUP_ROOT,
# launcher/startx.sh, launcher/profiles.py's PROFILES_ROOT by way of
# paths.py) working completely unmodified - confirmed live earlier that
# content placed under /var at build time really does survive to the
# real running system, so the source tree staged below into
# /var/lib/cosmos/app/current really will be there as
# /cosmos/app/current at runtime.
RUN mkdir -p /var/lib/cosmos && ln -s /var/lib/cosmos /cosmos

# paths.py's own real DATA_ROOT - a symlink into /var, same idea and
# same reason as /cosmos just above (a real top-level mountpoint
# isn't on bootc-image-builder's own customizations.filesystem
# allowlist - confirmed live: "path \"/userdata\" is not allowed" - only
# /var-rooted paths are). Confirmed separately that this is still safe
# for install_to_filesystem.py's own real, separate-partition install
# path: mounting a filesystem onto a symlinked target path resolves
# through the symlink and lands on the real underlying directory (a
# plain scratch mount --bind test against a symlink confirmed this
# directly), so that script's own `Where=/userdata` systemd .mount unit
# keeps working completely unmodified - the real partition just ends up
# mounted at /var/userdata transparently either way. chmod 777 on the
# real target matches paths.py's own documented reasoning (a fixed,
# world-writable directory, not derived from any particular real user
# account).
RUN mkdir -p /var/userdata && chmod 777 /var/userdata && ln -s /var/userdata /userdata

# The real installer for this platform - see its own module docstring
# for why `bootc install to-disk` doesn't fit (always wipes the whole
# target device) and `to-filesystem` plus this wrapper's own manual
# partitioning does. Baked into the image itself so it doubles as its
# own installer medium: `podman run --privileged <this image>
# install-to-filesystem.py <device>` against real hardware, no separate
# live ISO needed - install()/update_slot() from the superseded Debian
# pipeline used the same "the image is its own installer" idea, just
# via a live boot instead of a container run.
COPY atomic/install_to_filesystem.py /var/lib/cosmos/boot/install_to_filesystem.py
RUN chmod +x /var/lib/cosmos/boot/install_to_filesystem.py

# The real CosmOS source tree - staged by atomic/build_bootc_image.sh
# (which runs packaging/stage_build.sh first) into build/bootc-stage/,
# a plain `git ls-files` copy of everything tracked, committed straight
# into /var/lib/cosmos/app/current with no packaging step in between
# at all (CosmOS is no longer an AppImage, per direct request).
#
# wm/consolewm.c is recompiled fresh here rather than trusting whatever
# stage_build.sh may have already compiled on the host machine that ran
# it - a host-compiled binary isn't safely assumed ABI-compatible with
# THIS image's own libc/libX11, the same class of cross-environment risk
# packaging/build_appimage_in_debian_chroot.sh used to exist to dodge
# for PyGObject specifically. PyGObject/pycairo (and every other real
# pip dependency - packaging/requirements.txt) are pip-installed here
# too, straight onto this image's own system Python, built from source
# against this exact image's own GTK/GObject-Introspection headers -
# build-time and run-time are now the SAME image, so there's no ABI
# mismatch to worry about at all (unlike the old AppImage build, which
# ran on a separate Fedora dev/CI machine that could easily disagree
# with the target). Devel headers/gcc/libX11-devel are install-use-strip
# in this one RUN step, matching the NVIDIA driver build's own pattern
# above.
COPY build/bootc-stage /var/lib/cosmos/app/current
RUN dnf install -y \
        gcc \
        gtk3-devel \
        gobject-introspection-devel \
        librsvg2-devel \
        cairo-devel \
        cairo-gobject-devel \
        python3-devel \
        pkgconf-pkg-config \
        libX11-devel \
    && pip3 install --no-cache-dir -r /var/lib/cosmos/app/current/packaging/requirements.txt \
    && gcc -O2 -Wall -o /var/lib/cosmos/app/current/wm/consolewm /var/lib/cosmos/app/current/wm/consolewm.c -lX11 \
    && dnf remove -y gcc gtk3-devel gobject-introspection-devel librsvg2-devel cairo-devel cairo-gobject-devel python3-devel pkgconf-pkg-config libX11-devel \
    && dnf clean all

# The stable boot chain - deliberately NOT part of the swappable app
# tree above (see startx.sh's own docstring), same sibling-directory
# layout the now-retired Debian rootfs build used too (its own sed
# rewrites the debuglog.sh source path the same way).
RUN mkdir -p /var/lib/cosmos/boot
COPY launcher/startx.sh debuglog.sh /var/lib/cosmos/boot/
RUN sed -i 's#\$SCRIPT_DIR/\.\./debuglog\.sh#$SCRIPT_DIR/debuglog.sh#' /var/lib/cosmos/boot/startx.sh \
    && chmod +x /var/lib/cosmos/boot/startx.sh

# Getty-autologin-into-X - tty1, the real convention for a standalone
# OS image/VM (confirmed with the user: tty2 is only used on the real
# dev machine itself, to test without disturbing its own already-
# running tty1 desktop session - a real, from-scratch image like this
# one owns the whole machine, so tty1 is correct here). The profile
# content ships as a real /usr-resident template (direct build-time
# writes into /root fail outright - it's a dangling /var-backed
# symlink until boot) copied into place by ExecStartPre on every login
# attempt, not the ambient tmpfiles-setup service - two real,
# confirmed-live reasons, not one: it doesn't reliably run this rule on
# its own on this image, AND (found via a real `bootc-image-builder`
# raw-image boot, not the to-filesystem installer path this was first
# proven against) that image's own first-boot seeding already creates
# an EMPTY /root/.bash_profile before tmpfiles-setup ever runs - a
# tmpfiles "C" line only copies when the destination is missing
# entirely, so it silently no-ops against an existing empty file
# either way. A plain `cp -f` sidesteps both failure modes at once by
# not depending on "missing" semantics at all.
COPY atomic/bash_profile.template /usr/share/cosmos/bash_profile.template

RUN mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/getty@tty1.service.d
COPY atomic/getty-tty1-override.conf /etc/systemd/system/getty@tty1.service.d/override.conf

# Debugging convenience only, never for a real shipped image: autologin
# the serial getty too so this build's own boot can be interacted with
# over qemu's -serial socket while porting is still in progress.
RUN mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/serial-getty@ttyS0.service.d
COPY atomic/serial-getty-ttyS0-override.conf /etc/systemd/system/serial-getty@ttyS0.service.d/override.conf

# A real, confirmed-live upstream bug (bootc-dev/bootc#2402 and its own
# root cause): systemd-gpt-auto-generator discovers the ESP/boot
# partition independently of ostree-system-generator's own real
# `boot.mount` bind mount, and creates a COMPETING `boot.automount`
# (120s idle timeout) targeting the exact same /boot mountpoint. Once
# that automount re-triggers (any access to /boot more than ~2 minutes
# after boot), it stacks an autofs pseudo-mount on TOP of the real ext4
# one - confirmed live via `findmnt /boot` showing both. This isn't
# just cosmetic: `ostree-finalize-staged.service` (the thing that
# actually applies a staged `bootc upgrade` at shutdown) tries to
# `mount -o remount,rw /boot`, which fails outright against the autofs
# layer with "Remounting /boot read-write: Invalid argument" - so a
# real `bootc upgrade --apply` reports "Queued for next boot" and
# reboots, but the reboot lands back on the SAME old deployment with
# the fetched update silently orphaned (no boot loader entry ever
# written for it). Confirmed live, twice: first the failure exactly as
# described (`journalctl -b -1 -u ostree-finalize-staged.service`
# showing the exact error above), then the fix - `systemctl mask
# boot.automount` - verified end to end with a real upgrade AND a real
# rollback afterward, both landing on the correct, different digests.
# ostree's own real `boot.mount` is the only thing that should ever
# manage this mountpoint on an ostree/bootc system, so permanently
# masking the competing generator-created unit is correct, not a
# workaround for a specific test - not just a bandaid for the separate,
# narrower bug (`opendir(boot): Operation not permitted` on a plain
# `bootc status`/`upgrade --check` call under bootc's own `unshare -m`
# re-exec) already worked around directly in launcher/app.py.
RUN systemctl mask boot.automount

# A single, real, confirmed-live-but-benign AVC denial hit on every
# boot of the getty chain above: `login` sending a signal to the Xorg
# process startx.sh spawns, as part of login's own normal session
# teardown - denied under SELinux enforcing, but harmless (Xorg and the
# real launcher kept running stably regardless, confirmed multiple
# times). checkpolicy/policycoreutils are build-time-only - not removed
# afterward since Steam/Wine/Kodi's own combined size already dominates
# this image, not worth a multi-stage build just to shave a few MB.
# checkmodule's own naming rule (the output basename must match the
# module's declared name) is why this gets staged as /tmp/cosmos.te,
# not atomic/cosmos-selinux.te's own filename.
COPY atomic/cosmos-selinux.te /tmp/cosmos.te
RUN dnf install -y checkpolicy policycoreutils \
    && checkmodule -M -m -o /tmp/cosmos.mod /tmp/cosmos.te \
    && semodule_package -o /tmp/cosmos.pp -m /tmp/cosmos.mod \
    && semodule -i /tmp/cosmos.pp \
    && rm -f /tmp/cosmos.te /tmp/cosmos.mod /tmp/cosmos.pp

RUN bootc container lint
